[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for adding a hillshade layer that is driven by a **raster‑DEM source** to a MapLibre GL JS map.  
Everything is plain JavaScript (no extra plugins) and works with any MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0 (the current stable releases).

---

## 1. What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **A raster‑DEM tile service** (e.g. `https://{s}.tile.opentopomap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` or a custom MBTiles/Cloud‑hosted DEM) | The hillshade layer can only read DEM data from a source of type **`raster-dem`**. |
| **A MapLibre GL JS map** (already created with a style) | The hillshade will be added as a new *layer* on top of that style. |
| **CORS / access‑token** (if the DEM tiles are hosted on a different origin) | The browser must be allowed to fetch the tiles. |
| **Optional: a “terrain” source** (if you also want 3‑D terrain) | Not required for hillshade, but you can reuse the same DEM for both. |

---

## 2. Add the raster‑DEM source to the style

You can either:

* **Inject the source directly into the style JSON** (good if you control the style file), or  
* **Add it programmatically** with `map.addSource()` (easier when you’re building the map in code).

### 2‑a. Example source definition (JSON)

```json
{
  "id": "my-dem",
  "type": "raster-dem",
  "tiles": [
    "https://my-dem-server.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
  ],
  "tileSize": 256,
  "maxzoom": 14,
  "encoding": "mapbox"          // or "terrarium" – see note below
}
```

* **`encoding`** tells MapLibre how the DEM values are stored in the PNG.  
  * `"mapbox"` – the Mapbox DEM format (signed 16‑bit integer stored in the red/green channels).  
  * `"terrarium"` – the Terrarium format (unsigned 16‑bit integer stored in RGB).  
  * If you’re not sure, try `"mapbox"` first; most public DEM services use that.

### 2‑b. Adding it in JavaScript

```js
// Assuming `map` is your MapLibre GL JS instance
map.addSource('my-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  tiles: [
    'https://my-dem-server.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14,
  encoding: 'mapbox'   // or 'terrarium'
});
```

> **Tip:** If you also want a 3‑D terrain surface, you can reuse the same source:
> ```js
> map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
> ```

---

## 3. Add the hillshade layer

A hillshade layer is a **`type: "hillshade"`** layer that points at the DEM source you just created.

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hillshade',
  type: 'hillshade',
  source: 'my-dem',          // <-- the raster‑DEM source defined above
  layout: {},                // optional – usually empty
  paint: {
    // ---- Visual tweaks (all optional) ----
    // Light direction (0‑360°, 0 = north, 90 = east)
    'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,

    // Light intensity (0‑1)
    'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map', // or 'viewport'
    'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,          // makes shadows deeper

    // Color ramps (default values are fine for most cases)
    // 'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
    // 'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
    // 'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.2)'
  }
});
```

### What the paint properties do

| Paint property | Typical range | Effect |
|----------------|---------------|--------|
| `hillshade-illumination-direction` | 0‑360° | Sun azimuth. 315° = north‑west (common default). |
| `hillshade-illumination-anchor` | `"map"` or `"viewport"` | Whether the sun follows the map rotation (`map`) or stays fixed on the screen (`viewport`). |
| `hillshade-exaggeration` | 0‑2 (or higher) | Makes the relief look more dramatic. |
| `hillshade-shadow-color` / `hillshade-highlight-color` / `hillshade-accent-color` | Any CSS color | Tweak the darkness of shadows, brightness of highlights, and subtle accent shading. |

You can change any of these at runtime with `map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-exaggeration', 1.2);`.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hillshade from raster‑DEM</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
    center: [8.54, 47.38], // Zürich
    zoom: 12,
    // Optional: make CJK text render locally (not needed for hillshade)
    // localIdeographFontFamily: '"Noto Sans CJK SC", sans-serif'
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 2️⃣ Add the raster‑DEM source
    map.addSource('my-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      tiles: [
        // Example public DEM (MapTiler DEM tiles, free up to 100 k requests/month)
        // Replace with your own URL if you have one.
        'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 14,
      encoding: 'mapbox'   // MapTiler uses the Mapbox terrain‑rgb format
    });

    // 3️⃣ (Optional) Enable 3‑D terrain – comment out if you only want hillshade
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'my-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });

    // 4️⃣ Add the hillshade layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'my-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.6,
        // Uncomment to tweak colors:
        // 'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        // 'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a free API key from [MapTiler Cloud](https://cloud.maptiler.com/).*  
If you have your own DEM tiles, just swap the `tiles` URL.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Hillshade is missing / map shows a blank gray area** | The DEM source URL is wrong, CORS blocked, or `maxzoom` is lower than the current zoom. | Open the tile URL directly in the browser → check HTTP status. Add `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` on the server or use a proxy. Increase `maxzoom` if you’re zoomed in beyond it. |
| **Hillshade looks flat (no shadows)** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is `0` (default is `1`). | Set a non‑zero value, e.g. `0.5` or `1`. |
| **Colors look washed out** | The default `hillshade-shadow-color` / `highlight-color` may be too light for your base map. | Override them in the `paint` object. |
| **CJK labels disappear after adding hillshade** | You set `localIdeographFontFamily: false` (or omitted it) and the style’s `glyphs` URL does not serve CJK glyphs. | Either keep the default (`'sans-serif'`) or point `localIdeographFontFamily` at a local CJK font you have installed. |
| **Terrain 3‑D and hillshade look out of sync** | The hillshade source and the terrain source are different DEMs or have different `exaggeration`. | Use the **same** source for both (`map.setTerrain({source: 'my-dem'})`). |
| **Performance drops when zooming fast** | Too many DEM tiles are being requested (high `maxzoom` + large `tileSize`). | Reduce `maxzoom` to the highest you actually need, or increase `tileSize` to `512` if your server provides larger tiles. |

---

## 6. Going further

| Goal | How to achieve it |
|------|-------------------|
| **Add a second hillshade with a different sun direction** | Add another `hillshade` layer with a different `id` and a different `hillshade-illumination-direction`. |
| **Blend hillshade with a raster basemap** | Place the hillshade **below** the raster layer (`before: 'my-basemap'`) or use `raster-opacity` on the basemap. |
| **Show hillshade only at certain zoom levels** | Use the `minzoom` / `maxzoom` properties on the layer definition. |
| **Animate the sun (day‑night cycle)** | In a `requestAnimationFrame` loop, call `map.setPaintProperty('hillshade', 'hillshade-illumination-direction', angle)` where `angle` changes over time. |
| **Use a custom DEM (e.g. a city‑scale LiDAR raster)** | Generate PNG tiles in the **Mapbox terrain‑rgb** or **Terrarium** format, host them (e.g. via Amazon S3 + CloudFront), then point the `tiles` array at that endpoint. |

---

## 7. TL;DR – Quick copy‑paste

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the map (any base style works)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('load', () => {
  // 2️⃣ Raster‑DEM source
  map.addSource('dem', {
    type: 'raster-dem',
    tiles: ['https://my-server.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 14,
    encoding: 'mapbox'   // or 'terrarium'
  });

  // 3️⃣ Hillshade layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'hillshade',
    type: 'hillshade',
    source: 'dem',
    paint: {
      'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,
      'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.7
    }
  });
});
```

That’s it—your map now displays a smooth, GPU‑accelerated hillshade derived from the raster DEM you supplied. Happy mapping! 🚀
